A||||11||fropuff||What do I need for engine swap?||||1||
Z||000000||fropuff||07-06-2001||12:56 AM||||Hey guys.  As of now, I have an 80 Cutlass w/ a blown 231 V6 in it, sitting in the yard.  I'm going to need to look into buying a winter vehicle soon, and I'm wondering if I should keep the Cutlass or Enduro it.  I need a new engine..that's for sure.  I'm wondering how complicated it would be to swap in a Chev 350 CI.  Can I just get the engine itself, or do I need to change a lot of other sh*t?  If it's going to be difficult, I won't bother, and it's headed to the speedway.  Thanks for any help.<P>By the way, the car has:  a Buick 3.8L 231 C.I. 6 cylinder w/ a 3-speed auto tranny.  Thanks again!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Pontiaddict||07-06-2001||02:20 AM||||You will probably need a complete engine, and all the accessory brackets (possibly the accessories too), flywheel, trans (if it isn't a dual pattern bellhousing) motor mounts basically everything that's buick has to be replaced with chevy parts.<BR>If you find a buick V8, almost everything will bolt to it from the v6. You would still need a flywheel and some other stuff, but not as much as the chevy motor.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Pontiaddict||07-06-2001||02:23 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Pontiaddict:<BR><B>You will probably need a complete engine.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>By that I mean an engine with everything on it as it was running in another car. With all of the little things that you wouldn't need if you were just replacing another small block chevy v8 (such as a 305 to 350 swap)<P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Formula88||07-06-2001||09:54 AM||||The tranny bellhousing is a problem.  The Chevy won't bolt to a Buick tranny.  However, any B-O-P (Buick, Olds, Pontiac) V8 should bolt up to it.  Failing that, since you'll need to get a complete engine with accessories to go in, you might consider getting the tranny with the engine, too.  It's really no harder to swap the engine/tranny as a unit than just the engine.  Also, the V6 tranny likely won't hold up long behind a V8.  A good Turbo 350 tranny should hold up ok.||||reg||1
Z||000004||rogergarrison||07-06-2001||03:10 PM||||Unless its a sharp looking one, I say sat nite speedway for it. Those are cheaper than Fieros... around here anyway. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Archie||07-06-2001||07:04 PM||||Here is my 1984 Cutlass FWD Wagon with a Chevy V-8 in it.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Mvc-899s.jpg"> <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Mvc-900s_1.jpg"> <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Seanh||07-06-2001||07:30 PM||||Archie, do you put a 350 in all your vehicals? I'd bet if you had a Ford, you'd be one to put a Chevy 350 in it.<P>I have a complete Olds 403, about 100K on it, ran fine at removal. I'd let ya have it cheap, but if you had to ship it, the shipping cost would kill the good price of the engine. So if you close to Nebraska....||||reg||1
Z||000007||fropuff||07-07-2001||01:29 AM||||Sean: I'm wondering some more stuff on this engine.  As of now, I don't want to spend money on a crappy winter car.  I'd rather get an engine for mine, and a cheap engine is cool!  I was wondering if it has a good amount of HP/torque.  The old crappy 231 had no power at all; I had to floor it at every stop sign to get a decent take-off.  I spent $10 a day in gas.  I don't know much about the 403s, but if it's good, I might take it.  I'm also relatively close to Nebraska, up here in Minnesota.  I could get it in a weekend.  Before we go there, I need a little more information on this.  Thanks a lot!  Have a good one!<P>BTW: Archie, I wish I could put one of those little bastards in my Cutlass.  Sweet!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000008||fierospeeder||07-07-2001||03:14 AM||||I guess that beater Archie had on his lot has a secret in it.  <P>My mom just traded in her 88 chevy celebrity wagon which really close to the other wagons GM made.  I remember seeing Archie had one when i went to the car show at his business.<P>Front wheel burn outs.<P>the problems with that model and the 4 doors is the steel fuel line leaking (because of rust) near the fuel filter.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Seanh||07-07-2001||02:59 PM||||Front Wheel Burn-outs rule man!!<P>Hey Archie, if you read this again and get this message, how hard would it be to put a 350 SBC in my 81 Porsche 924 5spd? I wanted a new Porsche engine, but they are just about as foriegn to that car as a 350 would be, and 350 parts are a hell of alot cheaper. I mean good-god $65 for a Porsche distributor cap! $229 for 4 banger ignition wires!||||reg||1
Z||000010||fropuff||07-09-2001||09:51 PM||||Hey guys.  I talked to Sean and looked over some info, and the 403 puts out 185/320, maybe a little less.  The 403 is complete, minus the carb and exhaust manifold.  To swap a 403 into my Cutlass, I need motor mounts, belts, etc.  Would this be worth it?  Thanks!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000011||fropuff||07-11-2001||12:33 AM||||I did a little searching, and came up with this:<P>"80 Cutlass <BR>I had a 1980 Cutlass with a 403 stuffed in it. It ran 13.60s stock with <BR>street tires, well semi stock. <P>Stock rebuild moly rings Edlebrock intake 3711 Comp Cam's cam (I believe <BR>the one with the 433 lift and 262 duration) 650 dual feed double pumper <BR>Holley carb dual 2.5" Flowmasters TH-350 trans with shift kit and no <BR>stall (with stall I would burn up in the gate) 3.73 rear end <P>With that combo I beat almost every Chevy on the west side of Chicago <BR>and Ford Mustangs couldn't stay there either. Believe me with a light <BR>body and the right gear, you can't lose with a 403. It came stock with <BR>185 horses and 320 lb. of torque. They called me the green machine from <BR>Ogden and Kosner."<P>&gt;Sounds pretty sweet.  I just don't know how involved and expansive it'd be to do.  ??? ||||reg||1
